Message-ID: <35460E61.8EC2DE17@zk3.dec.com>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 348473740
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 7bit
References: <6i18ie$ao0$1@power42t.hkbu.edu.hk>
To: "P.M.Wong" <s11976@net1.hkbu.edu.hk>
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=us-ascii
Organization: CTG
Mime-Version: 1.0
Newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1


The acronym ATOM stands for Analysis Tools using Object Modification.
 That is, ATOM is a programmable instrumentation toolset that works by
 modifying executables and shared libraries to call a tool's routines that
 analyze the application's run-time behavior (eg. profiling it).

 ATOM is a system for building customized analysis tools. Software developers 
 can use this system to create their own program analysis tools. The ATOM 
 system includes all of the runtime libraries necessary for executing ATOM
 based analysis utilities and tools. The ATOM system for building cus-
 tomized analysis tools is licensed with Digital UNIX.

 DIGITAL has created several analysis tools that are built using the
 ATOM system. These tools are: Third Degree, Pixie, and Hiprof. All tools
 support shared libraries and threaded programs, and they are licensed with
 the Developers' Toolkit.

 The Third Degree tool checks for memory access errors and memory leaks.
 Pixie is a basic block profiler which supports profile-directed optimization
 and allows instruction-level execution counts to be obtained. Hiprof provides
 procedure call profiles of a program, similar to but more powerful than 
 gprof's sampled profiling information.

 ATOM and the above analysis tools are available with Digital UNIX V4.0 
 and later versions.
 
 Please send me email if you would like further information on these tools.

	John P Williams, Digital UNIX Development Environment Engineering.



