Subject: Lots of Threads = Death?
Date: 1998/04/28
Message-ID: <35464b25.29547822@149.174.211.108>
X-Deja-AN: 348574462
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 7bit
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=us-ascii
Organization: Strategis Consulting Inc.
Newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1,comp.unix.internals,comp.unix.programmer


Hi! I'm seeing some strange goings on with creation and destruction of lots
of threads.

I'm using a DEC Alpha system with 128Meg of RAM running 3.2G. Before the
start of each test, the system had approximately 9250 pages of free memory,
according to vmstat. As a test, I wrote a program that creates a thread
(pthread_create), passing it a value that came from the command line (int).
The initial thread then does a join on the thread it created, effectively
blocking until that thread terminates.

The newly created thread checks to see if the value it got is zero. If it's
zero, it just blocks on a gets() call. If it's > 0, then the thread creates
a thread and does a join on that thread. The new new thread is passed a
value one less than its parent received, and is instructed to start
executing the same function that its parent was executing.

This has the effect of causing N threads to be created, with no more than
one thread created by each thread.

I used vmstat to monitor the progress of this process' execution and the
system resources that were being used along the way. At an appropriate
point, after vmstat showed the system stable again, I press Enter,
satisfying the last thread's gets() call. It exits, causing its parent
thread's join to return. When the join returns, the thread exits. Thus, the
satisfaction of the gets() causes (eventually) all of the threads to
terminate. The initial thread, when its join returns, displays a message and
waits on another gets() call.

So, we've got something fairly simple here. A bunch of threads get created
and do nothing but create a thread and wait for it to exit. Eventually, the
waits are satisfied and all the threads exit, leaving just the initial
thread.

After running this program with just a few to a dozen threads, to verify its
correct operation, I opened it up. I tried 2000 threads. (We had earlier
tuned the kernel to allow 4000 threads per user.)

Watching vmstat's output, I saw pretty much what I expected to see. I saw
the number of waiting threads increase by 2000. I saw the amount of free
memory drop. I saw a lost of user and system cpu time. But, all the threads
got created in about 10 seconds. The system stayed pretty happy throughout
the several minutes I allowed it to sit there with the extra 2000 waiting
threads. Then, I hit the Enter key and watched the threads self-destruct.
First, I was surprised to see even more memory being used as the threads
exited. There was a spike in zero-fill page requests of at least one per
thread. Also, the thread destructions took significantly longer to finish
than their creation had. Another thing that surprised me was that the
available memory did not all return until the initial thread (and the
process as a whole) terminated. This implies that the pages allocated for
the threads were being allocated within the context of the process, and were
not being given back to the system until process termination.

Deciding to push the envelope a bit, I fired up my program for 2500 threads.
This behaved much like the 2000 thread case, except that it drove available
memory lower.

Based on something I'd seen in an earlier test, I pushed a bit further, to
2600 threads. This time, the number of free pages was reduced to about 100,
at which point it appears that a kernel process kicked in to attempt to
recover free memory space. After a while, it had managed to return the free
memory reading to a little over 3500 pages. During the time it was doing its
thing, however, the number of waiting threads changed. The number stopped
reflecting the 2600 threads in my program, returning to the value it had
before I started the test. When the memory recover operation concluded, the
number of waiting threads returned to its value indicating my waiting
threads. It's as though they vanished for a minute or so, then re-appeared.
They did not appear on either of the other two columns in vmstat.

I continued to push things until a run where I asked for 5000 threads. It
didn't make it. I got an error message where I ran the program:

$ ./tc 5000
Main Thread 5000 started - Thread ID= -1071781384
%DECthreads bugcheck (version V3.12-311), terminating execution.
% Running on DEC OSF/1 AXP [OSF1 alpha V3.2(62); cpu type 30,
	configured for 1
%  cpu, 1 cpu in box, 128Mb]
% Reason: set_noaccess: 12 protecting 0x8010000 to 0x8011fff
% See 'cma_dump.log' for state information.
Resources lost(coredump)

I found both a cma_dump.log file (about 4,700,000 bytes) and a (quite large)
core. According to the cma_dump.log, everything was going along pretty well
until I got to thread 3761. It's information from the log file is:

  Thread 3761 (running) "<pthread user@0x8007b40>" (0x7eb8018)
    Scheduling: throughput policy at priority 19
    There is 1 thread waiting to join
    Thread specific data: 1: 0x7ea3f88
    Stack: 0x800f788; base is 0x8010000, guard area at 0x8009fff
    General cancelability enabled, asynch cancelability disabled
    Current vp is 11285, synch port is 11286
      VP state is run: Mach policy throughput, priority 19 (RT kernel),
state
        running; synch port has 0 messages out of 1
    Join uses mutex 7598 and condition variable 7521; wait uses mutex 7599
and
      condition variable 7522
    The thread's start function and argument are 0x120013cc0 (0x4d8)
    The thread's latest errno is 12

So, it looks like I ran out of some kind of space for thread stacks. To try
to work around this, I set the thread attributes for thread creation to use
a stack size of 16000 (figuring I'd get 16K instead of 32K, allowing me
about twice as many threads). Although I can confirm that this value was
set, 32K was still given to each thread, causing my next test to blow up at
exactly the same point.

So, what's up? What am I running out of? What do I need to do to fix it?
What should I be doing differently, smarter, better?

Thanks!




