In article <6i4put$o65$1@news.kornet.nm.kr>, jeong wung kee <youyou@soback.kornet.nm.kr> writes:
|> Alpha 4100 with OSF 4.0b is running here. 
|> I have two questions in my mind. 
|> 
|> ONE IS : How can I enlarge the number of maxuprc which currently
|>          set to 64, should I rebuild kernel ?

It is not necessary to rebuild the kernel.  Complete instructions
for increasing the maximum number of processes per user are available at

    http://www.digital.com/internet/document/ias/tuning.html#max-proc-per-user

The document is about tuning Digital UNIX for Web servers, but you
may find that it provides useful information for other applications.


|> TWO IS : Is there a way to tell how many files are being open by 
|> 	 a user and is there any way to change the limit of the 
|> 	 allowance ?

The open-max-soft and open-max-hard parameters can be used to
limit the number of file descriptors per process (but not for
a specific user).  The value of open_max_soft is the default
per-process limit. A process can increase its soft limit up to
its hard limit using setrlimit.  See the "System Tuning and
Performance Management" manual for more information.

As far as I know, there is no easy way to tell how many files
a user has open.  You might be able to use the public-domain
"lsof" command, but I would not count on it.

-Jeff



