Digital UNIX does not set a block size for the tape drive like AIX
it looks like and AIX tape drive with block size set to 0.

I'll answer with more than you asked :-

Commands to get SCSI device info

as root
		file /dev/rmt*
eg
# file /dev/rmt*
/dev/rmt0a:     character special (9/5126) SCSI #0 TLZ07 tape #3 (SCSIID
#5) (SCSI LUN #0) 
/dev/rmt0h:     character special (9/5122) SCSI #0 TLZ07 tape #3 (SCSIID
#5) (SCSI LUN #0) 
/dev/rmt0l:     character special (9/5120) SCSI #0 TLZ07 tape #3 (SCSIID
#5) (SCSI LUN #0) 
/dev/rmt0m:     character special (9/5124) SCSI #0 TLZ07 tape #3 (SCSIID
#5) (SCSI LUN #0) 

Traditionally the a is alternet density, h is high, l is low and m is
medium 
if you have tape drive that matches this generally h is the only one to
use.
h should get compress l should not - I have not bothered with
non-compress in such a long
time I assume it still works :)

/dev/nrmt*	where n is a non-rewind device so that when you close the
device it's left
at the current position

# scu -f /dev/rmt0h
scu> show dev

