Paul Eggert wrote:

>That's a nice try, but it's not good enough, since it requires me to do
>steps (e) and (f) each time I send a message, and this takes extra time
>(and also I'm likely to forget).  I need the "From:" line to be set
>every time, consistently and automatically, the same way that I can set
>it with most other email clients.

"Most other email clients" I assume means PC ones.  Most all of the Unix
mail clients I've seen assume that you want to be your userid @ your
host for mail - quite correctly.  Only Netscape as far as I know allows
you to say exactly who you want to be, since that can be used from
anywhere.

I have made this change to happen on every outgoing email from my system
- but it was just that.  I edited the /etc/mail/sendmail.cf file to
replace every address with the one I wanted.  Very ugly, but it worked.

The other option is to have a reverse alias on your mail server.

