Jeff Denham wrote:
> 
> Dirk Grunwald wrote:
> 
> > each stack entry requires one or more 'pmap' entries. A pmap is a
> > range of virtual -> physical mappings that share the same acccess
> > permissions.
> >
> > Threads tend to chew up a lot of map entries because each heap uses
> > two protection areas (one that is writable, and the guard page that is
> > not accessable).
> >
> > You can duplicate the same effect by using 'mmap' to allocate
> > bazillions of pages, and make certain adjacent pages don't have the
> > same permissions.
> >
> > My guess is you're running over the limit of the number of pmap
> > regions you can have.
> >
> > I think that kernel parameter is called 'vm-mapentries'.  You should
> > see it's current setting if you do a 'sysconfig -q vm | grep -i map'.
> > You should be able to reconfigure it using /etc/sysconfigtab.
> 
> Also, why you're at it, increase the vpagemax parameter -- say double or
> tripleit. See the documentation on kernel tuning.

The original poster was asking about his DEC Alpha with D Unix 3.2g
Download the sys_check package from ftp://ftp.digital.com/pub/DEC/IAS/

It will make some recommendations. For my machine it recommended:

 vfs:
        name-cache-hash-size = 512
vm:
        vm-mapentries = 16384
        vm-vpagemax = 65536
        vm-ubcseqpercent=20
        new-wire-method=0

Caveat: My machine is running 4.0b

