Pui Ming WONG wrote:
> 
> Mark Gelinas (gelinas@bismuth.zk3.dec.com) wrote:
> : s11976@net2.hkbu.edu.hk (Pui Ming WONG) writes:
> 
> : > Could someone tell me which versions must i go thru in order
> : > to jump from 3.2a to 4.0d
> 
> : 1)  3.2a  -->  3.2c  -->  4.0  -->  4.0a  -->  4.0d
> 
> : 2)  3.2a  -->  3.2c  -->  4.0  -->  4.0b  -->  4.0d
> 
> : 3)  Backup all disks, do a fresh install of 4.0d directly,
> :     then restore all appropriate files.
> Really confusing ! Dunno what you mean by 3

you can do any of the three choices.  3 is the best especially
if you have been using advfs.  the upgrades leave all kinds of
garbage lying around that (in my case) prevented me from being
able to build a kernel when I used sequence 2 (above).  

You may not be able to do 3 though.  I did 3 on a big server
that was using advfs and the advfs data disks weren't compatible
with 4.0 advfs!  I couldn't access my advfs domains.  The
DEC hotline advised that I backup my data, rebuild the advfs
domains and restore my data from tape.  This would have taken
me at least 40 hours to do.



