Hi,

There are some of us planning the Unix track at DECUS in Los Angeles. 
During
the past year, we realized that DECUS has not been delivering what its
attendees wanted.  So around December, a small task force went forth to
put
together a kernel of presentations.

These presentations are to be oriented toward systems administrators,
not
managers or programmers.  They are to be task-oriented, to help you
install
and tune your system, not bulleted features of the next release.  And
most
important, they are to be given by the engineers who make the product. 
We had
planned this for Philadelphia, but got the word out too late.  We intend
on
presenting this in Los Angeles, where we will have even more
presentations
that previously planned.

We have a base of seminars and sessions, but are interested in what
else people would like to see, particularly in the way of
user-contributed sessions.

There is a majordomo list set up for people who wish to help plan
Unix activities at DECUS, or who just want to see what is being planned.
To join, just send mail to "majordomo@zk3.dec.com" and put the words
"subscribe decus-planning" in the body (not the subject line) of the
mail.  Then you will be subscribed to the list.  If you wish to
unsubscribe at a later time, you can just send the words
"unsubscribe decus-planning" the same way, and majordomo will
unsubscribe you.

To date, this is what is being proposed for DECUS:

There are nine seminars:

	o six given by ISA (a "professional" educational group that works
		closely with engineering, and often gives training sessions
		to Digital engineers for either new hires or engineers who
		are not familiar with that aspect of the product), these six
		will be "hands-on with equipment".  The course titles below
		are approximate.

	Brad Nichols - TruCluster Installation and tuning
        Ken Pugh - TCP/IP Installation and tuninig
        Jane Montiquila - System Admin - installation and tuning
        Bill Moloney - NT UNIX Integration
        Dean Brock - Storage for Unix systems
        Pravin Mundkur - Web Server - Installation and tuning

	o two given by IAPS (another "professional" organization).

	Tom Doeppner - Digital Unix Internals
	Robert Seacort - Common Desktop Environment integration

	These two seminars are lecture only

	o one given by Rochelle Lauer on Clustering:  This one will be on
		clustering in general, and more of how to DESIGN your cluster
		rather than on installing and tuning it


The sessions offered by engineering are task-oriented, technical
presentations,
not just bulleted features.

Here is a list of the ones offered now, each being 80 minute sessions
(to allow
for time to get started and time to wrap up, with lots of meat
inbetween:

        DIGITAL UNIX Strategy & Roadmap (this was shortened to 30
minutes)  		
