thomas@beagle.bmc.uu.se (Thomas Sicheritz) writes:

>I try to mount a directory from an Dec OSF1V4.0  machine on a linux box
>2.0.32, with: mount -t nfs -o soft -v host_a:/path  /local_path

>I receive: mount: wrong fs type, bad option, bad superblock on host_a:/path,
>or too many mounted file systems
>       
>       If I check the logs:
>       NFS server host_a not responding, still trying.
>       nfs_fhget: getattr error = 512
>       nfs_read_super: get root inode failed

My first reflex would be to run tcpdump and watch the message flow.  (The
Digital UNIX FAQ has a summary of what you need to know to install the
packet filter and use tcpdump.)

Barring that, I'd triple check that the Linux box has the right IP
address for the DEC box.  I don't know if Linux is trying to use NFS
over TCP (which was introduced in Digital UNIX in V4.0).  You might
try forcing Linux to use UDP or checking that the DEC side is running
nfsd with the -t option.  Actually, you can check the latter via
"rcmgr get NUM_TCPD".

The 512 error doesn't sound like DEC error code we'd return, see what Linux
has to say about that.

