The release says:

     The proposed settlement would resolve these concerns by requiring
    Digital to enter into or to continue certain licensing arrangements
    with AMD and Samsung Electronics, or some other Commission-approved
     licensee, and to begin the process of certifying IBM or some other
   Commission-approved company to manufacture the Alpha chips. The order
    would require that the licenses be full "architectural" licenses --
   that is, licenses not only for the production of Alpha chips, but for
   the Alpha architecture itself along with some sublicensing rights, so
       that these licensees can innovate and develop their own Alpha
   microprocessors, as long as they maintain backward compatibility with
   the existing Alpha architecture. The purpose of these provisions is to
   establish two licensees and another manufacturing source as providers
    and developers of Alpha devices, independent of Intel. According to
     the Commission, these consent provisions provide solid protections
   that would ensure that Alpha remains a viable competitive alternative
                             to Intel's chips.

The release seems confusing, saying Digital must  "continue certain 
licensing arrangements" with AMD and Samsung and also enter into a 
foundry agreement with another company, most likely IBM, with 
licensees being full architectural licensees to ensure that there 
are "two licensees and another manufacturing source as providers
and developers of Alpha devices, independent of Intel."  However,
AMD only wants the bus, and probably couldn't afford to do Alpha
development.  Plus it lacks enough capacity for its own products at 
this point.  So if the FTC wants two architectural licensees,
the new manufacturing partner might have to become it.  

At the same time the Register (www.theregister.co.uk) is
claiming that AMD wants to sell the K6 to IBM and break
the company up.  

: So, has Alpha become the Merced of AMD? I.e., will AMD push ahead with
