In article <6hokii$hfp$1@nntp.Stanford.EDU>,
Jonathan Stone <jonathan@DSG.Stanford.EDU> wrote:
>AMD is at least poised to use the EV7 bus as their answer to Intel's
>Slot-1 manipulation of the x86 market.  If motheboards that're
>pin-compatible with Alphas become mass-market commodity items,
>Digital/Compaq and Samsung and AMD and customers all win.

I sure hope so.  I think people have been overblowing the disadvantages
of non-Intel-compatible motherboards, they always point to the failure of
NexGen to penetrate the market but personally the only reason I didn't buy
one was because you couldn't have both L1 cache and the FPU at the same time,
not the oddball board, and I'll bet I'm not alone in that.

Only twice have I been able to upgrade an Intellish CPU w/o having to swap
mobos anyway (8088 => V20 and 486 => 5x86), every other time there's been
something, pinout change or new voltage requirements or whatever, that's
forced me to replace the board at the same time as the CPU.  Even without
incompatibilities, usually by the time you're ready to upgrade the new
boards have some cool feature that makes them worth the price anyway --
snazzy integrated I/O controllers, the latest fad video slot, temp sensors,
async PCI or whatever.  I hope all the magazine authors will realize this and
resist the urge to scare people with pro-Intel hype.  Motherboard upgradability
mattered back when the boards actually cost anything significant, but not now.

John Wilson
D Bit



