>> This is true.  And there might actually be an advantage with the
>> K7 using the 21264's "bus", since it offers a much higher bandwidth
>> than the P6 bus will.  So if the K7 has a good fp engine, it could
>> really shine.  But it doesn't sound like the K7 will be "pin"
>> compatible with the 21264, since they will use different packaging.
>
>  There were some rumours flying around that at least some 21264's would
>  be packaged in a slot-A cartridge like the K7.  If this is true then
>  the K7 and 21264 could be pin compatible and interchangeable.  On the
>  other hand, extracting 60+ W from a cartridge the size of a slot 1
>  SEC might be a bit on the challenging side so it might only be feasible
>  for "low" speed binned 0.25 um 21264 parts.
>

People seem to be getting this mixed up, the Slot-A was designed by DEC for
the 21264, and is going to be used by AMD for the K7. The whole point is
that the 2 CPU's will have compatible electrical interfaces. The only thing
that should need changing is the firmware (which are all FLASH these days).

However you will almost certainly get 21264's using other (older)
interfaces, as DEC has been pretty good in the past letting customers
upgrade their systems wihtout throwing half of them away.  Most of the older
Alpha Servers (ie. 2100) have the CPU on a daughter card, which also
controls the clock speed of the CPU.







