Michael Gros wrote:
> 
> Hi,
> 
> It's seems like older sendmail (SMTP) would allow a
> HELO command without a domain name. The lastest sendmail
> off the DEC site does not allow HELO without a domain name
> and does not let the remote domain connect. I have noticed
> that there are a half dozen domains trying to connect to
> me this way, and one directly complaining to me.
> 
> My advise to them so far it to fix their SMTP so that a
> domain name is presented with the HELO command.
> 
> Any comments on what used to be the norm, what's changed,
> and why?

RFC 1123 requires a fully qualified hostname after HELO (or EHLO).
Beginning with sendmail 8.7, sendmail enforces this unless you have
enabled the AllowBogusHELO option in your configuration file.

Your advice is correct. If you want to be lenient, you can enable the
option to let the other admins delay the inevitable.
 
-Tom 
