I just found this amusing tidbit of information on ComputerWire's
website:

   The FTC left no doubt that its unanimous decision to approve Intel
   buying DEC's Alpha foundry and manufacturing the chip for DEC was
   contingent on DEC and AMD finalizing their deal. The agency also wants
   still another second source for Alpha in addition to AMD and Samsung
   and it appears that it expects DEC to license IBM to manufacture the
   widgets. Ironically, back in 1991, before DEC went and built the
   famous Hudson plant that Intel will be buying, IBM approached DEC with
   the notion of being its Alpha fab and DEC rebuffed it. If DEC had
   taken IBM up on its offer, it would have avoided getting strung out
   over Hudson and the expense it took to run it.

Still, the FTC is out of their minds if they think having four Alpha
fabs rather than three is going to make a differance.  Plus you have
to take into account the costs for bringing up the processor on
each fab's line.  Splitting the market for Alpha processors
four ways will likely result in no one making any money from
the Alpha.

 




