In article <6ghcjc$pbv$1@nnrp1.dejanews.com>, wli@cincom.com writes:

>I wonder if there is a quick and easy to solve my problem. I have a DEC Alpha
>Unix box running with 4.0D. For some reason, I installed DEC UNIX 4.0B system
>on another disk on the SAME box.
>
>1) is there a quick and easy way to duplicate the accounts and file systems
>setting (we have two AdvFS on the 4.0D environment) on the 4.0B system? How
>can I mount the "existing" AdvFS file systems on this 4.0B system?

Check the manual page for advscan(8).  I think it does what you want.
Of the domains were touched by the later installation, getting them
back is little more than creating a directory for each domain and
creating the links to the devices.

>2) is there some way to mount the file systems (e.g. / and /usr) on the 4.0D
>disk in the 4.0B environment?

It depends precisely on what you're trying to do.  If you want them
for references to copy files over or to merge semi-customized files,
just mount the file system somewhere.  I do this a lot since I have
system disks for most previous versions I keep for testing.

You want to use them directly, then no, you can't.  A large part of
what a version V4.0D or V4.0B is the statically linked applications
and the run-time libraries.  Mixing V4.0D applications and a V4.0B
kernel is not wise.

>3) if I have to setup the AdvFS systems again on the new 4.0B system, can I
>preserve the existing files/data on the file systems

Again, see advscan.  If you let it find the old domains and filesets
you'll be ok.  Don't run mkfdmn or mkfset to create the domains and
file sets, since that will overwrite the disks.


