>Michael Daly wrote in message <352B711F.EF9D886D@caaisn.leeds.ac.uk>...
>>I want to upgrade from Digital UNIX 4.0B to Digital UNIX 4.0D. The
>
>I can't help any but would like to ask you what the advantages are in being
>at 4.0d that impel you to upgrade.  My v4.0b system just went into
>production about a month and a half ago, and I'm loathe to try my first OS
>upgrade on it, but I know someday I'll have to.  Is 4.0d the one to learn
>on, and is now the time?  Are there advantages to using 4.0d?  Are there
>software compatability issues?

The main reason people are upgrading from 4.0B to 4.0D is Y2K
compliance. It's claimed that 4.0D performs better than 4.0B, having
been rebuilt with a new compiler technology - I can't vouch for claimed
figures of up to 15%, but the machines I upgraded did seem a bit more
responsive. They also chuck in some new extras on the Associated
Products CD, including a 2-user copy of ASDU (the successor to
Pathworks).

It also carries many (but not all) of the bugfixes from 4.0B, but be
aware that the first aggregate ECO kit has been posted to
www.service.digital.com !

As regards software compatibility - I'm not aware of any major issues
with 4.0D but I'm not using much beyond IASS and a few items from the
Associated Products CD's.

The upgrade is relatively straightforward, and can be achieved in about
half-an-hour to an hour - if you can spare that sort of downtime, it's
worth the effort.

Chris
