> Schlaatzweg 1, D-14467 Potsdam (Germany)     Fax: +49-0331-27537-98
> ------------------------------------------------------------------------

Dear Dirk,

	you might read 'man SCSI'. There it is explained that for a disk
with SCSI id = n at bus number = m is: d = n + 8 * m.
Exerpt:
  The unit number is a combination of the SCSI bus number (either 0, 1, and
  so on) and the device's target ID number.  The unit number is eight times
  the bus number plus the target ID.  For example, an RZ26 disk at target ID
  3 on bus 0 would be referred to as rz3; a TLZ04 tape at target ID 5 on the
  second SCSI bus would be referred to as 13.
I hope this helps. You should be able to figure out on what bus your controller
is with 'uerf -R 300 | more' (read the latest startup message), or by examaning
your system config file. So your number should be just 3, or 11, or 19, or..

By the way, I heard from my DEC technician that more than one PCI controller
is NOT supported by the xxxau machine. Under heavy load there might be
data corruption on such systems! Just check that your configuration does
not make troubles.

Yours, Hans.
