Subject: Re: how to prevent ICMP redirects
Date: 1998/04/01
Message-ID: <6fsubj$k94$1@star.cs.vu.nl>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 339781258
References: <6fqvnl$mo6$1@nnrp1.dejanews.com>
Organization: Fac. Wiskunde & Informatica, VU, Amsterdam
Newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1


Hi!

: Systems with two interfaces each--one fddi and one ethernet--are growing
: huge route tables over time.  Flags indicate routes are being learned
: via ICMP redirect.  Systems are not running any dynamic routing protocol
: (routed or gated).  Is there a way to prevent routes learned by ICMP
: redirect from being written into the routing table?

If I understood IP right, there is no chance of getting rid of ICMP redirect.
It's part of the IP/ICMP protocol suite.

But: ICMP redirect packets are created by routers whenever they get a packet
from one of your dual-homed hosts which has to leave the router on the same
port the packet got in. In fact, for the networks in charge of this problem,
you should choose different (default) routers in your static routing entries.
That way, you won't get no more ICMP redirect packets.
And as a side effect, you would statically perform the routing optimization
that by now your systems learn dynamically via ICMP redirect.

Anyhow: What is wrong with large routing tables? Do you e.g. suffer from
performance degradation?


Thilo Kielmann
