Allen M. Cohen wrote:
> 
> We get many unaligned access errors from a AP when built under 
> Dec Unix 4.0-D and as:
> "cc -non_shared -Wf,_p8 -O -std -Olimit 3000"
> When we build with as just "cc -g", there are no unaligned access
> errors!  Is this a compiler bug?
  
  It's very likely a problem in your application. If you post a small
  snipit of code that shows the problem, we can help you understand
  what's causing it.  

  When you compile with "cc -g", all optimizations are disabled. 
  These include optimizations that take advantage of the C language
  rules that guarantee the alignment of various datatypes.  That's 
  probably why the unaligned errors go away with "cc -g".

> Should I use uac to ignore unaligned access errors?

  I wouldn't.  Unaligned access errors degrade performance.
  Much has been written about unaligned access errros.  It's usually
  a matter of taking the pc reported by the unaligned access error
  and looking at the source code at or around that pc to see what's
  causing it.

  Below are pointers to two web pages which might be of help.
 
See
http://www.partner.digital.com/www-swdev/pages/Home/TECH/techsupport.html

  Choose "Frequently Asked Questions" then "DIGITAL UNIX"

or 

http://www.partner.digital.com/www-swdev/pages/Home/TECH/faqs/dunix/unalign.html

BTW, I see you're compiling with "-Wf,_p8" and "-Olimit 300".  Neither
switch is recognized by DECC.  As of UNIX V4.0, the -Olimit switch is
only recognized by -oldc.  I've never seen the _p8 switch. They're not
doing any harm, nor are they doing any good.

Joe Mario



