Hello,
	Machine in question: an Alpha 400 4/233, 160MB RAM, 4.3GB swap
partition, with OSF v4.0 and Sendmail 8.7.5.  Users are not allowed
shell accounts.
	I've been asked to resolve what would seem to be a Sendmail issues on
this machine that is serving email for aprox 6,000 users.  If it weren't
for not finding anything at all relating to the issue in ALL the
Sendmail information that I've been through, I would not be spending
bandwidth here, however . . . 
	Intermittently, it seems that the Sendmail daemon is refusing
connections.  On the client end, when mail is sent, a message is almost
immediately returned that it cannot connect to the mail server.  It pops
up so fast that it doesn't even seem like packets are being dropped; 
more like they are being refused.  Other than that message when clients
send mail, no other information is found, nothing in log files, or on
console.  Sometimes after about 5 minutes, things are okay.  Other
times, it does not come back and after a reboot things are okay for
another 4-5 hours.
	While this is going on, I can ping the machine, ftp and/or telnet to
it, hit the web site being served there, and seemingly anything else I
want.  It seems to be *just* sending mail.  BTW - I can also POP mail
from that box when this is happening (just no sending).
	When I first looked at the system there was 128MB RAM in it.  I bumped
it up to 160MB to no avail.  I then looked in the mail queue . . . HOLY
LEFTOVER MAIL!!  There was just under 3GB of queue there.  After several
hours of clearing it out, I was sure that this had to be the problem . .
. unfortunately not.
	This problem exists both for people dialed up to the service and to
machines on the same ethernet segment that use this guy for mail.  
	I hope I haven't left out any big details.  I am at a total loss for
where to look next.  Any suggestions?  Have any of you seen these same
issues with OSF??  I didn't find anything at all like this in sendmail
info.  Any assistance will be greatly appreciated!  BTW - I've had
Sendmail running on both a Slackware box and a Solaris box for quite
some time too, and haven't seen anything even close to this there.

-- 
Regards,
Ryan Gray



