"Hakan Aysan" <94fha@eng.cam.ac.uk> writes:

> Well- if you think that voice dictation will not become commonplace within
> the decade, you are wrong- wait and see. If available and working properly,
> would you really not use it over a keyboard (this isn't a sarcastic
> question, I'm genuinely interested since I know of no-one else who would,
> and I am studying speech processing at the moment at University).

I would prefer a keyboard for most things:

1) In an office environment, talking out loud might not be the best
way to interact with a computer (consider if one is sending email to a
headhunter, or working on a confidential memo, or simply the noise
factor in a cube farm).

2) For dictating a memo, speech might be useful.  For programming, it
might be a lot harder.

3) The spoken language and the written language are often very
different.  People use colloquialisms in spoken language that they
don't when writing.

4) When one is sick, it's often much easier to type than to talk.

5) We know a lot about the long term effects of typing, and a lot of
research has gone into mitigating those ills.  We don't know about the
long term effects of talking all day, especially if people have to
modify their diction even slightly to be understood.  At the very
least, I think this will delay the universalization of voice
dictation.

