Well, having recently not talked to Intel, I think I can add a few
thoughts.  Intel has been working on collecting all the magic smoke
from their earlier chips that are now dying in their long term test
facilities and repackaging it.  Since they (indeed, the entire
industry) now use much more advanced production processes, they can
repackage the magic smoke much more densely than it was originally
packaged.  Please note, however, that there are still some fairly
major limitations to this technique.  First, magic smoke remembers how
many bits it was designed for so currently Pentium IIs can only use
magic smoke from 386 and later chips.  (Currently, Intel is working on
a process whereby they can combine the magic smoke from 2 32 bit chips
to put into a single 64 bit chip (ie Merced)).  Also, it must be
remembered that magic smoke for older chips was designed with larger
particle sizes (because of the larger feature sizes on earlier chips -
remember 386s were running on a 1.5 micron process), thus the magic
smoke may not be suitable for all future applications.  Fortunately, a
novel solution has been found to this problem - by using specially
shaped mirrors to reflect the smoke they can make it appear smaller to
the chip (think about funhouse mirrors here - they make you look
smaller, but you actually stay the same size).

But Intel believes that all these technical hurdles can be worked
around (indeed, they already have worked around most of them).

But, the big remaining issue is with the EPA.  As you know by now, the
EPA is allowing some companies to sell pollution 'credits' to other
companies (basically, pooling a bunch of companies into one for the
purpose of measuring pollution).  However, the current law was
designed only to facilitate company to company transfers of pollution
credits; Intel is now petitioning the EPA to allow them to sell these
pollution credits to private citizens (after all, the new chips are
basically smoke with a few mirrors thrown in).

Jon
who didn't actually check all the math himself, so doesn't know if any
of it is true or not.




