Yes, more per MHz is an interesting issue. 
Personally I have a thing for my own benchmark metric, which is 
performance per transistor-clock. I care far less for a machine which can
do some large amoutn of work by brute force, than i do for a machine
which might get less work done but far more efficiently with a clever design.
I find it ludicrous that we are looking at so-called RISC processors
which boast millions of transistors. 
In my years, i have a few systems which have earned a great deal of 
respect, and incidentally have the highest 'performance per transistor-clock'
scores as well: namely, the R2000/R3000, and the Sparc 1. 
The R3000 has the highest such score. For a processor that simple, and at
such low clock speeds, it gets _damn_ good performance. 
(the sparc gets kudos for being more scaleable and having slightly
fewer gates, but the r3k does outperform it a bit) Now granted, this is 
just using simple benchmarking routines, but it makes a point. We have
gotten to the point where we are paying ridiculous prices in complexity and
clock rate for minimal gains in real performance. Processors today are
huge and have incredible clocks, it is true, but they are so grossly inefficient
at using those gates and those cycles, it is disappointing to me. 
We should look more into clever design and efficient use of the silicon,
not just the brute force result! 


