On Wed, 8 Apr 1998 11:25:42 GMT, kilgallen@eisner.decus.org (Larry
Kilgallen) wrote:

>In article <352A8A18.B41C348E@rhea.umd.edu>, "Jeffrey J. Potoff" <jpotoff@rhea.umd.edu> writes:
>
>> Why are you comparing performance per clock cycle ?  It's completely 
>> irrelevant.  What matters is performance per dollar.
>
>Performance per dollar matters in some applications.

Nope. Reality speaking here, It matters in _all_ applications.

>In other applications absolute peak performance (independent of cost)
>matters most.
>
>There are probably applications where performance per BTU of heat
>dissipation is the most critical metric.

The most visible form of this app is called a "laptop".

>While I agree that performance per clock cycle is pretty meaningless,
>I think it unwise to name any single other metric as the sole relevant
>statistic.

Yah, like "absolute peak performance (independent of cost)"
Such an application does not exist. If it did, we'd already
have an infinite-flops machine and everyone would be dead.
Which is of course the absolute peak in performance without
concern for cost. ;-)

Ops/$ compares performance better than any other form of measure
that I have seen. It does so because it's what everyone really
uses when they decide to buy a machine.

Folks that can't think very well will attempt to retort with
something like: "Well, I can buy a 386sx40 for one dollar, so it
beats your xxyyzz modern cpu to bits on mips/$". I answer: "OK,
quote me a price for a 10specfp99 machine based on your 386sx40
chips." They always become quiet. Sure a machine with that kind
of performance _could_ be built out of 386s, but it would cost
more than the one built out of PPro200s, or P2s, or AXPs.

Ops/clock in combination with ops/$ can be applied as a measure
of design and silicon efficiency. The more ops you can get out
of each transistor, as more transistors take more silicon, and
faster ones cost more, and more advanced processes can make them
faster, smaller, and cheaper all at the same time...in the end
it still ends up as performance/dollar whether you get a high
ops/clock ratio at relatively low MHz or just higher clocks.
Do you want to rate your processor on perf/transistor, perf/clock,
clock rate, or power consumption alone? No, you rate it on perf/cost.

No one in their right mind would buy a machine just because
its perf/clock ratio is higher than another.

It really boils down to this: Given two boxes, both can do the
job, one costs less to purchase _and_ to use. Which do you buy?

Given the same two boxes, one has a higher perf/clock ratio than
the other. Which do you buy? It's a pretty inane question isn't it?




