Gregg E Economou wrote:
> 
> Yes, more per MHz is an interesting issue.
> Personally I have a thing for my own benchmark metric, which is
> performance per transistor-clock. I care far less for a machine which can
> do some large amoutn of work by brute force, than i do for a machine
> which might get less work done but far more efficiently with a clever design.
> I find it ludicrous that we are looking at so-called RISC processors
> which boast millions of transistors.
> In my years, i have a few systems which have earned a great deal of
> respect, and incidentally have the highest 'performance per transistor-clock'
> scores as well: namely, the R2000/R3000, and the Sparc 1.

  The millions of transistors RISC processors you find so ludicrous got that way
  by sucking up a lot of the little bits and pieces scattered all over the CPU
  board of your R3000 and "Sparc 1" computer.  You know, little miscellaneous
  pieces like FPU (R3010 and Weitek 1164/65 chipset respectively), cache, and in
  the case of SPARC, an MMU.  I guess the benefits of lower overall silicon cost,
  fewer chip crossings, higher speeds, lower power consumption, less board area,
  lower parts count, lower packaging cost, and much higher reliability arising from
  this integration pale in comparison to buggering up your metric of performance
  per transistor-clock.   ;-)

> The R3000 has the highest such score. For a processor that simple, and at
> such low clock speeds, it gets _damn_ good performance.

  It didn't seem so simple to the poor engineer who had to get the funky external
  instruction and data caches working with the R3000's double pumped interleaved
  data/instruction bus (Your neat little 25 MHz R3000 effectively ran its cache
  interface at 50 MHz)  I seem to recall accounts that the cache clock tuning,
  deskewing, and duty cycle control needed for reliable and manufacturable R3000
  systems were quite non-trivial engineering exercises at the time.   MIPS was
  ill suited  for embedded control until IDT brought out parts like the R3041 and
  R3081 that hid this complexity by bringing it on chip.

> (the sparc gets kudos for being more scaleable and having slightly
> fewer gates, but the r3k does outperform it a bit) Now granted, this is

  Early SPARC gets kudos for fewer gates at the cost of leaving out an MMU?  I
  guess we wouldn't want the guys porting OS's to have only a single memory
  management model to worry about.  Sun had other reasons for making this area
  ambiguous and not having their own hardware follow the "SPARC reference MMU"
  design (and people thought Intel was so creative coming up with Slot 1 to
  hinder clones)

> just using simple benchmarking routines, but it makes a point. We have
> gotten to the point where we are paying ridiculous prices in complexity and
> clock rate for minimal gains in real performance. Processors today are
> huge and have incredible clocks, it is true, but they are so grossly inefficient  ^^^^

  Then:  MIPS R2000  :   80 mm2    (no cache or FPU)
         MIPS R3000  :   56 mm2           "
         SPARC-1     :   64 mm2    (no cache, MMU, or FPU)
         (MB86901)

  Now:   R4300       :   43 mm2
         604e        :   47 mm2
         Pentium MMX :   98 mm2

> at using those gates and those cycles, it is disappointing to me.
> We should look more into clever design and efficient use of the silicon,
> not just the brute force result!

  Well maybe if we were stringing 74LS00's together to implement these complex
  beasts your concerns with gate count would be more commendable.  Transistor
  counts without consideration of the regularity factor doesn't impress me
  much as I spend my days working on chip designs with many tens of millions of
  transistors.  For example I wouldn't be suprised if the 2.1 million transistor
  SA-110 StrongARM CPU is actually a simpler design to conceptualize than the
  120,000 transistor R3000.

[snip]
> you want a benchmark? Look at how many clocks a processor takes to complete
> various primitive operations. artihmetic, branching, etc.
> though I also would take into accoutn how much silicon is being used to
> do it as well, and give the smaller chip more credit for doing more with
> less.

   You don't consider how those old chips only contained a small fraction of
   the logic needed to implement a full featured system with MMU, cache and
   FPU.

   Your argument taken to its logical extreme would put the highest praise
   on the SSI ECL chips in the Cray-1 that contained a single NOR gate or
   flop.  After all, those chips only took a handfew of transistors to
   implement yet comprised a hugely powerful (for its day) computer system.

   
   A big factor to consider is that CPUs and main memories have been on completely
   different performance vs time curves the last 20 years and this affects overall
   system characteristics normalized to some metric like MHz or transistor count.

   For example, compare a 25 MHz R3000 system with 150 ns DRAM main memory
   against a 500 MHz Alpha with 60 ns DRAM main memory.  Which one looks
   less efficient per MHz for memory intensive code?  Are you suprised?   

> 
