Bryan Bahnmiller <bbahnmil@redwood.dn.hac.com> writes:

> All mounts
>continued to use NFS V3. We then specified "proto=udp". The number of
>NFS errors has declined! I feel as if we are now using the shotgun
>approach to tuning and troubleshooting.

TCP is supposed to provide better performance in lossy environemnts, but
I've seen several cases where it makes things worse.  The most recent
involved a network where some 100BaseT hard was configured for full duplex
and hooked to other hardware that was configured for half duplex.  I suspect
that the increased bidirectional traffic (for TCP ACKs) resulted in a
higher collision/loss rate or however xxBaseT is supposed to handle that.

For a while I was getting pretty good performance, but then I discovered
that the routing daemon was sending all my messages through a Solaris
system that was doing routing and not the show router.  That configuration
allowed unidirectional traffic between the misconfigured hardware and it
survived much better.

I think it may be more accurate to say that NFS over TCP has advantages in
_congested_ networks.  Lossy networks can cover many situations that TCP
isn't able to help on.  (FTP or ttcp should encounter similar problems.)

	-Ric Werme
